  "## What this records

A closing transaction for the session that advanced the clj->kotoba refactor.
It exists so the next agent can resume without the conversation. Read the
`in flight` section first -- six background agents were launched minutes before
this was written and their work is NOT accounted for below.

## The migration work that landed

The task began as `make the kotoba CLI + amu native build actually work`. The
blocker was not that native did not work -- it did -- but that every call
re-verified the artifact, so `nx/execute` cost 1.9-3.6 s. Splitting it into
`prepare` / `invoke` / `close!` in `kototama.native.executor` (tender-native
ab76044, 1915ee1) brought it to 47 ms per call, with `admit-validity!`
re-checking expiry, not-before, and revoked signers per invoke so the split
does not buy speed by dropping a check.

On top of that, a delegation seam was proven across five repos: an
`oracle.cljc` that loads a shipped `*.kir.edn` and executes an export, with no
fallback, plus a test-only generator and two gates (drift, delegation). The
reference implementation is `cloud-itonami-app`'s `kotoba_oracle.cljc`
(98a57c6); calendar, crdt, fsm and app-files reuse the template.

### The boundary, measured rather than assumed

Delegation is bounded by the interpreter's ADT node limit, not by whether a
value is a collection. `mokuroku` refuses 13 catalog items; `fsm` accepts 32
events over an alphabet of 3. The axis is whether the value's SIZE GROWS WITH
THE DOMAIN. An earlier note in this series said `a collection cannot be
delegated`; that was too strong and is corrected here.

Value shapes at `ir/execute`, measured by running the guests: records cross as
`[schema field ...]` in declared order; variants as `[schema case-tag payload]`;
a typed set as `[[:set :keyword] [items...]]` -- NOT a Clojure set; `[:option
:string]` as `[[:option :string] true \"x\"]`; `:document` as `[\"vector\"
[[\"i64\" 50] ...]]`.

### Defects the seams exposed

Writing an oracle forces someone to state what a function means, and that is
where the bugs were:

- `calendar/model.cljc` `overlaps?` accepted zero-duration intervals (fixed,
  d565658)
- `app-files` declared a confinement rule and never enforced it -- `BrowseSource
  -fetch` returned rows outside the granted directory (fixed 4d4a7bd, bound to
  its core by a gate 6572f6c)
- `net-kotobase` treasury: `js/parseInt` returned NaN on an unparseable block
  header, NaN propagated, and a payment that was never confirmed was accepted
  as confirmed -- the Worker returned success where it owed a 402 (fixed
  dfa1143, with a test failing 5 assertions against the old copy and a
  finite-head positive control that passes against both)
- `nexus-x402`: a vendored header named `8a77e516` while the content was
  `848158de` (fixed 6ca13bf, comments only)
- `animeka`: a `:dev` alias `:local/root` named `orgs/kotoba-lang/
  murakumo-generation`, which west never creates -- it resolved only for whoever
  had the pre-rename directory as a leftover checkout (fixed 0c14939)

## The gate discipline worth carrying forward

`app-files`' gate states, per case, the directory each row is IN -- authored,
never computed with the implementation's own `parent-of`. Deriving it would
make the gate a mirror of the implementation: it would agree with ANY
implementation, including a wrong one. Written down, it fails when the real rule
stops recovering from a raw path what the author says the path means. Proven in
three directions (guest always-true: 14 failures; always-false: 12; host
replaced by `starts-with?`: 6, on exactly the descendant and root cases).

## Three fleet detectors, and the errors in them

`scripts/verify-{namespace-collisions,duplicate-registrations,vendored-copies}
.cljs` were built this session. An independent verification pass reproduced all
three and found real defects in two. Both have one shape: a report that reads as
an all-clear when the check did not run.

- `--check --verify-remote` FAILED OPEN. `gh` exits non-zero on an expired token
  and on rate limiting; `github-id` read every failure as nil, so a broken `gh`
  produced `0 CONFIRMED` and exit 0 -- a clean fleet reported in front of 40 real
  duplicate registrations. Now three outcomes: CONFIRMED / REFUTED / UNVERIFIABLE,
  with UNVERIFIABLE failing `--check` on its own. Measured with a `gh` on PATH
  that exits 1: old exits 0, new exits 1.
- `verify-namespace-collisions` walked only `<repo>/src` -- the THIRD instance of
  one error this session. 3,528 -> 3,837 repos (4,828 source trees), 8 -> 11 real
  collisions. The owner sets were wrong where it mattered most: `treasury/
  core.cljc` listed 5 owners with 7 on disk, `pay/x402.cljc` 4 with 6, and both
  missing copies included the one another ADR calls the worst in the fleet.

The narrow-scan error recurred three times in one day, each time in a different
script, twice after being corrected. Prose does not prevent it. What did catch
it was running an independent verification pass over the detectors themselves.

Also corrected: the claim that every duplicate-registration candidate was
confirmed one upstream with no forks. Two candidates are DIFFERENT repositories
sharing a root commit (`org-w3-svg` + `svgraph`; `com-unity-ads` +
`com-unity-api-services`). Retiring either on the offline signal would have
deleted a real project. Both were on disk when that claim was written.

## Duplicate registrations: 35 -> 1

The premise changed under measurement. Resolving all 4,162 west entries through
the GitHub API by repository id (strictly stronger than the on-disk scan) leaves
exactly ONE repository registered at two paths: `kotoba-lang/compiler` +
`kotoba-lang/amu`, id 1297097065. A rename campaign is actively draining the
rest. The other 39 root-commit groups are ORPHAN CHECKOUTS -- directories left
by renames whose west entry is already gone. West will never recreate them.

## In flight -- NOT accounted for above

Six agents were launched immediately before this closing and are still running.
Each may land a merge in a child repo. **A child merge without a west pin
advance leaves the fleet reading a stale tree** -- the exact failure this session
spent the day fixing. The next agent must check for their results and advance
pins.

1. ~~widen `verify-vendored-copies`~~ -- **DONE, merge 2d9a2de.** 24 -> 31 files
   across 4 orgs. No verdict changed for any of the 24 already reported, so
   nothing was lost by widening. `grep -rl VENDORED` finds 32; the one the scan
   correctly does NOT report is a file that merely DOCUMENTS the vendored copy
   beside it -- header matching now starts at the VENDORED token so a
   `copied from` sentence elsewhere cannot be read as a provenance claim.

   Four of the newly-visible files (`cypher/`, `graphql/`) declare no pin and
   put their provenance INSIDE the `ns` docstring, where the `start at the first
   (ns` subtraction cannot reach -- so the header counts itself as drift. They
   are permanently STALE by design (the namespace rename is deliberate,
   ADR-2608039970) and the report now says so on the line rather than letting
   the number read as code drift. `no pin declared` is also now distinct from
   `pin unverifiable`: never named one is not the same as named one we could not
   read.

   Caveat the agent raised and I am keeping: CURRENT is measured against each
   library's LOCAL CHECKOUT HEAD, which is only as fresh as the last
   `west update`. Three libraries were behind their pins on disk; zero commits
   touched any measured file in between, so the numbers hold at the pin too --
   but that had to be checked, not assumed. Same class as the other three
   checkout-vs-repo errors this session.
2. ~~refresh `wasm-webcomponent/.../kami_host.cljc`~~ -- **DONE, merge 7d78c84,
   pin advanced.** Refreshed byte-for-byte to `dbc25ebc6`. Safe because the
   upstream change is purely additive 3D support: the `ns` requires are
   unchanged, no new reader conditionals or capabilities, new vars sit in the
   portable section, and extra keys in the WebAssembly import object are ignored
   by a guest that does not declare them. 12/12 suites, 299 assertions, identical
   before and after -- which proves nothing broke, NOT that the 3D works, since
   no test covers the new surface; that was checked by hand against the rebuilt
   artifact instead. A covering test would have to be nbb (CLAUDE.md forbids new
   `.mjs`) and is larger than the refresh.

   Two things found on the way. The old pin `367b4a141b94` was **not an ancestor
   of kotoba's main** -- it sat on a side branch and only happened to have the
   same content as what landed. A vendored header can name a commit off the
   mainline and nothing notices. And the checked-in ESM artifact is **not
   byte-reproducible**: rebuilding from UNMODIFIED source already moves 85 lines
   of Closure symbol renaming, because shadow-cljs resolves ClojureScript from
   Maven at build time while its own version is lock-pinned. Only 23 of the 86
   changed lines are attributable to the change.
3. ~~investigate the three new `kami/ongaku/e2e/*` collisions~~ -- **RESOLVED:
   they were FALSE POSITIVES of my own widening, and the detector is now fixed.**

   `test/e2e/src` is a source root by name and ships to nobody: it is reachable
   only through each repo's own `:e2e` alias, and tools.deps does not export a
   dependency's aliases. Measured with `clojure -Spath -A:e2e` in the one repo
   depending on two of the three -- neither tree appears. The three files are
   also not copies: pairwise line similarity is 0.08-0.30 and no exported var
   name overlaps. They are three different programs that share a path.

   The fix narrows by DECLARATION, not by depth: a source tree counts when the
   repo's own `:paths` / `:source-paths` names it. `:extra-paths` is excluded on
   purpose. Narrowing by depth instead would have lost
   `net-kotobase/kotobase-api-gateway-cljs/src`, which is nested AND declared and
   holds the copy this detector exists to find. Measured after the change:
   ongaku entries 3 -> 0, `treasury/core.cljc` owners still 7 (the original
   narrow scan said 5), real collisions back to 8 with correct owner sets. A repo
   that declares nothing keeps every tree -- unknown must read as `look`, not as
   `skip`.

   **Still OPEN, and the obvious fix was measured and rejected.** The collision
   detector still walks only directories named exactly `src`, so 21 `src-*`
   trees holding 51 Clojure files are invisible to it -- among them
   `wasm-webcomponent/src-cljs/vendor/kotoba/kami_host.cljc`, whose namespace
   `kotoba.kami-host` is also defined in `kotoba-lang/kotoba/src/`.

   I tried making the DECLARED roots the source of the list rather than a filter
   over a `src`-named scan, which would have ended the naming question outright.
   It regressed, measurably: some repos declare `:paths [\".\"]`
   (`kami-genre-base-systems`' sub-projects), which makes the repo root a source
   root and manufactures namespace paths that carry their own prefix --
   `src/kotoba/lang/package_registry.cljc` appeared as a finding. Duplicate-
   registration suppression also collapsed from 19 to 4, because newly-visible
   trees added a third owner with a different root commit and broke the
   all-owners-share-a-root test. Reverted rather than landed.

   **CLOSED, merge `3d5a70ad627`. And my diagnosis of the regression above was
   wrong.**

   The declaration is now the list itself, with two guards -- both found by
   measurement, neither by reasoning:

   - **A declared root must lie inside the repo.** `net-kotobase` declares 28
     roots that resolve through `../..` into OTHER repositories
     (`orgs/kotoba-lang/pay/src`, `.../kotobase/src`). Taken at face value they
     make `net-kotobase` an owner of `pay`'s files, and the extra owner brings
     its own root commit. **That -- not the `same-upstream?` shape I blamed --
     is the actual mechanism behind the 19 to 4 collapse.** I proposed
     re-deriving suppression per owner pair; the real defect was upstream of
     suppression entirely.
   - **A declared root must not be the declaring directory.** `:paths [\".\"]`
     is legitimate and documented where it appears: `kotoba-lang`'s own deps.edn
     has it so `io/resource` finds `lang/cli.edn`, and the 20 `games/*` ones
     have it for `clojure -M author.clj` on files that carry no `ns` form.

   All five acceptance criteria hold, and the DIVERGENT list is byte-identical
   to before -- same 8 namespaces, same owners, same shas -- while the scan grew
   from 4,539 trees to 5,005 and from 14,867 namespace paths to 16,164.

   `kotoba.kami-host` is now visible, and it is **divergent but NOT
   co-classpath** (no closure reaches both owners), so it lives in `--all`. That
   exposed a real hole: divergent-not-co-classpath findings appeared in **no
   mode at all**, and they are 216 of 243. They now have their own `--all`
   bucket.

   `wasm-webcomponent` is the sharpest illustration of what the name filter cost:
   what it has named `src` is shadow-cljs's `:esm` OUTPUT directory. The filter
   did not merely miss two real source trees -- it selected the build product
   and discarded both.

   **One thing was implemented, measured, and deliberately reverted.** Per-owner-
   pair suppression satisfies four of the five criteria and holds suppression at
   19, but it drops `treasury/core.cljc` entirely. The reason is a finding about
   the detector rather than a bug: among treasury's 7 owners, the ONLY pair any
   closure reaches together is `gftdcojp/cloud-murakumo` and
   `network-awai/cloud-murakumo` -- one repo at two paths. So the finding is
   promoted by a relationship the script itself classifies as NOT a collision,
   and survives suppression only because owners that took no part in promoting it
   disagree about upstream. Per pair, the same pair decides both halves and it
   vanishes. `Restrict same-upstream to co-classpath participants` suppresses it
   too, so per-pair suppression and keeping treasury are mutually exclusive on
   today's data.

   That was written above `same-upstream?` in the source instead of being fixed
   by tuning a number: **CO-CLASSPATH is not the axis that finds
   `treasury/core.cljc`.** Catching it on purpose needs a second axis this script
   does not have, and `verify-vendored-copies.cljs` is the likelier home for it.

   Left undone, and named rather than silently absorbed: the three repos deviate
   from their own family convention. `plugin-host` and `project` scope their E2E
   namespaces (`kami/ongaku/<subject>/e2e/`), as does the `org-w3-webaudio`
   harness all five cite as their model; `sampler` dropped the segment at 00:25
   on 2026-07-13 and `sequencer` and `notation` copied its shape within the
   hour. Renaming is mechanical and needs no pin changes, but it is three repos'
   worth of churn for a naming inconsistency that now costs nothing.
4. ~~check `net-kotobase`'s `pay/core.cljc` + `pay/x402.cljc` for drift~~ --
   **RESOLVED, benign, no change made.** All three vendored copies are
   pin-honest. `pay/core.cljc` and `treasury/core.cljc` are byte-current;
   `pay/x402.cljc` is stale by exactly one strictly-additive commit (`848158d`,
