### The cleanup pass that caused the leftovers is fixed -- merge `a4556402485`

`:additive` is the one class `cleanup-land.cljs` auto-merges, and it merged on a
single argument: no path of this name exists on the default branch, so no
existing line can be rewritten. **A directory rename destroys that argument
precisely** -- the moment `worker/` becomes something else, every old path stops
existing on `main`. That is what a rename MEANS. A stale copy left at the old
address therefore satisfies `:additive` perfectly, and the existing guard, which
checks whether the same path is on base, is structurally blind to it.

`scripts/rename_residue.cljs` asks history instead of the disk, since on disk
residue and WIP are identical. Three signals: was this path a recorded rename
SOURCE reachable from base; are these bytes already in the object database; was
an ancestor directory moved away wholesale. The third handles the `.gitignore`
case -- a generated file is in nobody's history, so the first two miss it, but
following its dead parent forward through the rename chain (two hops in the real
repo) reaches a live directory where `git check-ignore` excludes it. **A file the
repo declares it ignores at its live address is not WIP at its dead one.**

It excludes only the provable half, with no silent-drop path: `:residue` is
dropped but reported by name WITH the live path (the bytes are still in the
object database, so nothing is lost); `:suspect` -- path dead but content NOT in
history, i.e. possibly a real edit against a stale copy -- is demoted to a draft
PR rather than discarded; `:wip` is untouched. Working-tree files are never
deleted.

Proven against the real incident: the tree parked at `8f7fbaf^` with all 17
leftovers restored classifies 17 residue, 0 suspect, 0 wip, and end-to-end the
plan drops from 20 files to the 2 that are genuinely new plus 1 demoted to
review. False-positive stress with 40 new files in live directories of the same
496-rename repo gives 0 residue.

Two things from it worth keeping beyond this fix:

- **43 of 121 `orgs/kotoba-lang/*` checkouts do not resolve `<remote>/main`** --
  west fetches into `refs/west/*`. A gate reading liveness from that ref would
  have sat silent across a third of the fleet. Liveness now comes from the base
  tree `cleanup-land` already fetches from the API.
- Registered as fleet gate `root-rename-residue`, and confirmed red two ways
  before landing. **This check's failure is invisible** -- a silent classifier
  still exits 0, still prints a plan, still merges, and the only symptom arrives
  after the next rename. It took six days to notice last time.

Also settled: `manifest/cleanup-workflow.md` has **no generator**. It and the
`.edn` have been hand-edited together in every commit that touches them, and the
edn calls it `:reference-md`, not a projection.

### `sdk-py` landed too -- merge `01fab5c46f7`. Pin BLOCKED, same limit as vpn.

The credential disclosure was confirmed, not merely inferred: with a canary key
and the transport stubbed, `chat` sent `authorization: Bearer <canary>` to
`http://levi.local:4000/v1/chat/completions`. The second site was confirmed
narrower exactly as suspected -- `default-request` hardcodes only
`content-type`, and the stub signature has no header slot at all, so what leaks
is query bodies and a trusted reply.

**`levi` and `simeon` are murakumo mac-mini fleet nodes** (`scripts/fleet-ci/
nodes.edn`) -- private LAN/tailnet hosts. That reframes the class: these are not
typos, they are a developer's real machine names shipped as library defaults.

The fix requires both endpoints and refuses rather than substituting an owned
one, for two reasons worth keeping. There is no public endpoint serving a
LiteLLM proxy on :4000 or an mst-projector on :8765, so any owned name would be
an INVENTED address. And for `llm.cljc` an owned default would not close the hole
anyway -- `ETZHAYYIM_LLM_KEY` belongs to the operator's proxy, so sending it
anywhere WE picked still discloses it.

The existing env reads were kept and none was added. They are this SDK's
pre-existing, README-documented configuration channel and the only input these
zero-arg functions have -- not an ambient read quietly introduced to replace the
removed constant, which is the thing #3235 stripped from yoroshiku's twin. The
distinction matters and the commit states it.

**The caller check is the part worth carrying forward.** No production caller
depended on the default, but **nine pre-existing tests did, silently** -- they
queried without ever naming a host and passed against the `simeon.local`
fallback. One of them, `test-400-error`, started passing for the WRONG reason
once the endpoint became required, because a config error is also a
`::mst-projector-error`. A test suite can encode a dependency on a bad default
so quietly that removing the default is what reveals it.

17 tests / 47 assertions baseline; 7 failures against unfixed source; 25 / 92
green after.

